        'title' => 'Eating Disorder Spectrum & Pediatric Feeding (Pica, Rumination, ARFID)',
        'slug' => 'eating-disorder-spectrum-pediatric-feeding',
        'description' => 'Pediatric feeding disorders in DSM-5: Pica, Rumination Disorder, and Avoidant/Restrictive Food Intake Disorder (ARFID) without body image distortion.',
        'overview' => "In DSM-5, Feeding and Eating Disorders were consolidated into a unified diagnostic chapter that spans the entire developmental continuum from infancy through mature adulthood. This restructuring integrated historically segregated pediatric conditions (Pica, Rumination Disorder, and Feeding Disorder of Infancy) alongside classical adolescent and adult eating disorders (Anorexia Nervosa, Bulimia Nervosa, and Binge-Eating Disorder).\n\nA fundamental diagnostic breakthrough in DSM-5 was the introduction of Avoidant/Restrictive Food Intake Disorder (ARFID), which replaced and expanded the poorly defined 'Feeding Disorder of Infancy or Early Childhood'. ARFID captures individuals of all ages who exhibit clinically significant food restriction or avoidance driven by sensory sensitivity (e.g., texture, smell), lack of interest in eating, or fear of aversive consequences (such as choking, vomiting, or pain), resulting in persistent failure to meet nutritional needs.\n\nCrucially, what distinguishes pediatric feeding disorders (Pica, Rumination, and ARFID) from Anorexia Nervosa and Bulimia Nervosa is the complete absence of body image distortion. In ARFID, Pica, and Rumination Disorder, there is no fear of weight gain, no drive for thinness, and no disturbance in the perception of body weight or shape. Eating restriction is driven by sensory, motor, or conditioned fears rather than cosmetic body dissatisfaction.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Diagnostic Formulations for Pediatric Feeding Disorders:\n\n1. Pica: Persistent eating of nonnutritive, nonfood substances over a period of at least 1 month (e.g., clay, dirt, chalk, paper, hair, paint). The eating behavior is developmentally inappropriate (minimum developmental age of 2 years to exclude normal toddler oral exploration) and not part of a culturally supported or socially normative practice.\n\n2. Rumination Disorder: Repeated regurgitation of food over a period of at least 1 month. Regurgitated food may be re-chewed, re-swallowed, or spit out. The behavior does not occur exclusively during anorexia, bulimia, or ARFID, and is not attributable to an associated gastrointestinal or medical condition (e.g., GERD, pyloric stenosis).\n\n3. Avoidant/Restrictive Food Intake Disorder (ARFID): An eating or feeding disturbance (e.g., apparent lack of interest in eating; avoidance based on sensory characteristics; concern about aversive consequences of eating) manifested by persistent failure to meet appropriate nutritional and/or energy needs, associated with one (or more) of the following:\n• Significant weight loss (or failure to achieve expected weight gain in children).\n• Significant nutritional deficiency.\n• Dependence on enteral feeding or oral nutritional supplements.\n• Marked interference with psychosocial functioning.\nCRITICAL EXCLUSION: There is NO evidence of a disturbance in the way in which one's body weight or shape is experienced, and no drive for thinness.",
        'specifiers' => "ARFID is specified: 'In remission'. Clinicians specify the primary behavioral driver: sensory avoidance, fear of aversive consequences (e.g., post-choking phobia), or lack of appetite/interest in eating.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "ARFID vs. Anorexia Nervosa: The definitive board distinction. In Anorexia Nervosa, food restriction is driven by intense fear of gaining weight and body image distortion; in ARFID, there is NO fear of weight gain and NO body image distortion.\n\nPica vs. Cultural Geophagy: In certain West African and Southern United States cultures, eating kaolin clay during pregnancy is a culturally normative practice; cultural practices are excluded from Pica.\n\nRumination vs. Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD): GERD involves involuntary acid reflux with burning pain; rumination involves effortless, voluntary or habitual regurgitation of partially digested food that is often described as pleasurable.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves an 8-year-old child who choked on a hard candy six months ago. Since that choking incident, the child has refused to eat all solid foods out of intense terror that swallowing will cause fatal choking (fear of aversive consequences).\n\nThe child consumes only strained liquids, has lost 12 pounds (dropping from the 50th to the 5th percentile for weight), and requires liquid nutritional meal supplements. The child desperately wants to be bigger and stronger and is distressed by their weight loss (no drive for thinness). Meeting criteria for food avoidance with severe weight loss and supplement dependence without body image distortion confirms Avoidant/Restrictive Food Intake Disorder (ARFID).",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: ARFID replaces Feeding Disorder of Infancy and applies to all ages; it requires nutritional failure/weight loss WITHOUT body image distortion or fear of weight gain. Pica requires eating nonfood substances for at least 1 month with minimum developmental age of 2 years (exclude cultural geophagy). Rumination Disorder involves repeated regurgitation for at least 1 month. If body image distortion or fear of weight gain is present, the diagnosis MUST be Anorexia Nervosa, never ARFID.",
