    2 => [
        'title' => 'Anorexia Nervosa (Restriction, Fear of Weight Gain, Low Body Weight, BMI)',
        'slug' => 'anorexia-nervosa-criteria-bmi-severity',
        'description' => 'Significantly low body weight, intense fear of gaining weight, body image distortion, elimination of amenorrhea, and BMI severity tiers.',
        'overview' => "Anorexia Nervosa is a severe, potentially life-threatening psychiatric disorder characterized by persistent restriction of energy intake leading to significantly low body weight, intense fear of gaining weight or becoming fat, and a disturbance in the way in which body weight or shape is experienced. Anorexia Nervosa carries the highest mortality rate of any psychiatric disorder (estimated at 5% to 10% per decade), resulting from lethal medical complications of starvation (cardiac arrhythmias, electrolyte collapse) and suicide.\n\nA landmark structural revision in DSM-5 was the complete elimination of the historical amenorrhea criterion. In DSM-IV, postmenarcheal females were required to have missed at least three consecutive menstrual cycles to receive an anorexia diagnosis. DSM-5 eliminated this requirement because amenorrhea cannot be applied to males, prepubertal females, postmenopausal women, or females using oral contraceptives, and research proved that menstruating and non-menstruating patients share identical biological and psychological morbidity.\n\nFurthermore, DSM-5 standardized the definition of 'significantly low body weight' using objective World Health Organization (WHO) Body Mass Index (BMI) guidelines. In adults, significantly low weight is defined as a BMI below 18.5 kg/m2 (or in children and adolescents, a BMI-for-age percentile below the 5th percentile). DSM-5 also established formal dimensional BMI severity tiers to guide level-of-care hospitalization decisions.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A: Restriction of energy intake relative to requirements, leading to a significantly low body weight in the context of age, sex, developmental trajectory, and physical health. Significantly low weight is defined as a weight that is less than minimally normal or, for children and adolescents, less than that minimally expected.\n\nCriterion B: Intense fear of gaining weight or of becoming fat, or persistent behavior that interferes with weight gain, even though at a significantly low weight. (Note: The inclusion of 'persistent behavior that interferes with weight gain' ensures patients who deny subjective fear of fatness but persistently starve themselves meet criteria).\n\nCriterion C: Disturbance in the way in which one's body weight or shape is experienced, undue influence of body weight or shape on self-evaluation, or persistent lack of recognition of the seriousness of the current low body weight.\n\nDimensional BMI Severity Tiers in Adults:\n• Mild: BMI >= 17 kg/m2.\n• Moderate: BMI 16 - 16.99 kg/m2.\n• Severe: BMI 15 - 15.99 kg/m2.\n• Extreme: BMI < 15 kg/m2 (indicates immediate medical emergency hospitalization).",
        'specifiers' => "Diagnostic Subtypes (evaluated based on the past 3 months):\n1. Restricting Type: During the last 3 months, the individual has NOT engaged in recurrent episodes of binge eating or purging behavior (i.e., self-induced vomiting or the misuse of laxatives, diuretics, or enemas). Weight loss is accomplished primarily through dieting, fasting, and/or excessive exercise.\n2. Binge-Eating/Purging Type: During the last 3 months, the individual HAS engaged in recurrent episodes of binge eating or purging behavior (i.e., self-induced vomiting or the misuse of laxatives, diuretics, or enemas).\n\nCourse Specifiers: 'In partial remission' or 'In full remission'.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Anorexia Nervosa vs. Bulimia Nervosa: The fundamental diagnostic boundary. If an individual is at a SIGNIFICANTLY LOW BODY WEIGHT (BMI < 18.5), the diagnosis MUST be Anorexia Nervosa (Binge-Eating/Purging Type), NEVER Bulimia Nervosa. Bulimia Nervosa requires maintaining body weight at or above minimally normal levels.\n\nAnorexia Nervosa vs. ARFID: In ARFID, there is NO fear of gaining weight and NO disturbance in body image; in Anorexia, fear of weight gain and body image distortion are mandatory.\n\nAnorexia vs. Medical Starvation (Hyperthyroidism, Malignancy, Celiac Disease): In medical cachexia, patients desire to regain weight and do not fear fatness.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 17-year-old student brought to the clinic weighing 84 pounds at a height of 5'5\" (BMI 14.0 kg/m2, representing Extreme Severity). The student restricts intake to 400 calories daily, runs eight miles every morning, and asserts that their thighs are 'disgustingly obese'.\n\nWhen confronted with vital signs showing bradycardia (heart rate 38 bpm) and hypothermia, the student laughs and insists they are in perfect health. Meeting criteria for energy restriction, significantly low weight, intense fear of fatness, and lack of recognition of seriousness, the clinical formulation confirms Anorexia Nervosa, Restricting Type, Extreme Severity (BMI < 15), requiring immediate medical inpatient stabilization.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Anorexia Nervosa requires: (1) significantly low body weight (BMI < 18.5), (2) intense fear of gaining weight, and (3) body image distortion. DSM-5 eliminated the amenorrhea requirement. BMI severity tiers: Mild (>=17), Moderate (16-16.99), Severe (15-15.99), Extreme (<15). Subtypes are Restricting vs. Binge-Eating/Purging based on the past 3 months. If a patient purges but has a BMI < 18.5, the diagnosis is Anorexia Nervosa, NEVER Bulimia Nervosa.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'The Significantly Low Weight Gateway', 'icon' => '⚖️', 'definition' => 'BMI < 18.5 in adults or < 5th percentile in children forms the non-negotiable gateway separating Anorexia from Bulimia.'],
            ['title' => 'Elimination of Amenorrhea Mandate', 'icon' => '🚫', 'definition' => 'DSM-5 removed the requirement of missing 3 menstrual cycles, enabling diagnosis in males and oral contraceptive users.'],
            ['title' => 'Four-Tier BMI Severity Scale', 'icon' => '📊', 'definition' => 'Mild (>=17), Moderate (16-16.99), Severe (15-15.99), and Extreme (<15 kg/m2).'],
            ['title' => 'Highest Psychiatric Mortality Rate', 'icon' => '🚨', 'definition' => 'Carries a 5-10% decadal mortality rate from cardiac arrhythmias, refeeding syndrome, and completed suicide.']
