    3 => [
        'title' => 'Anorexia Subtypes: Restricting Type vs. Binge-Eating/Purging Type',
        'slug' => 'anorexia-subtypes-restricting-binge-purge',
        'description' => 'The 3-month behavioral window, restricting mechanics vs. purging mechanics, electrolyte risks, and crossover trajectories.',
        'overview' => "Anorexia Nervosa is categorized into two distinct clinical subtypes based on the individual's eating and compensatory behaviors over the preceding three months: the Restricting Type and the Binge-Eating/Purging Type. This subtyping provides vital prognostic and medical risk information, as patients in the binge-eating/purging subtype carry significantly higher rates of medical mortality, electrolyte collapse, impulsive personality traits, substance use disorders, and suicide attempts compared to pure restricting patients.\n\nIn the Restricting Type, significantly low body weight is achieved and maintained purely through dietary restriction, severe fasting, and/or compulsive physical exercise. During the preceding three months, the individual has not engaged in recurrent binge eating or purging behavior.\n\nIn the Binge-Eating/Purging Type, the individual regularly engages in binge eating, purging, or both, while maintaining a significantly low body weight (BMI < 18.5). Purging methods include self-induced vomiting, misuse of laxatives, diuretics, or enemas. Diagnostic crossover between subtypes is exceptionally common over time: longitudinal studies demonstrate that roughly 50% of individuals with restricting anorexia eventually cross over into the binge-eating/purging subtype as physiological starvation drives uncontrollable binge urges.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Subtype Diagnostic Criteria under DSM-5 (Evaluated over the preceding 3 months):\n\n1. Restricting Type: During the last 3 months, the individual has not engaged in recurrent episodes of binge eating or purging behavior (i.e., self-induced vomiting or the misuse of laxatives, diuretics, or enemas). This subtype describes presentations in which weight loss is accomplished primarily through dieting, fasting, and/or excessive exercise.\n\n2. Binge-Eating/Purging Type: During the last 3 months, the individual has engaged in recurrent episodes of binge eating or purging behavior (i.e., self-induced vomiting or the misuse of laxatives, diuretics, or enemas).\n\n(Note: An individual who restricts intake, maintains a BMI < 18.5, and purges after eating small or normal amounts of food—even without true objective binges—is classified under the Binge-Eating/Purging Type).",
        'specifiers' => "Severity grading (Mild to Extreme BMI) is applied identically to both subtypes. Remission specifiers ('In partial remission' or 'In full remission') reflect recovery of normal weight and body image.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Anorexia, Binge-Eating/Purging Type vs. Bulimia Nervosa: This is a classic board examination trap. Both conditions involve bingeing and purging; however, if the patient's BMI is below 18.5 kg/m2 (significantly low weight), the diagnosis is ALWAYS Anorexia Nervosa, Binge-Eating/Purging Type. Bulimia Nervosa can be diagnosed ONLY if body weight is at or above minimally normal levels (BMI >= 18.5).\n\nRestricting Anorexia vs. Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder: Restricting anorexics often exhibit rigid rituals around food (cutting food into tiny pieces, counting peas); however, these rituals are driven by calorie restriction and fear of fatness, whereas OCD rituals are driven by contamination or magical thinking.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 19-year-old college student weighing 92 pounds at 5'6\" (BMI 14.8 kg/m2, Extreme Severity). Over the past three months, the student has severely restricted daytime calories, but twice weekly consumes 3,000 calories of ice cream and cookies, followed immediately by self-induced vomiting.\n\nSerum chemistry reveals severe hypokalemia (potassium 2.6 mEq/L) and metabolic alkalosis. Because the patient engages in recurrent binge eating and purging while maintaining a significantly low body weight (BMI < 18.5), the diagnosis is Anorexia Nervosa, Binge-Eating/Purging Type, Extreme Severity (NOT Bulimia Nervosa).",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Subtypes are determined by behavior over the past 3 months. Restricting = weight loss via fasting, dieting, exercise with NO bingeing/purging. Binge-Eating/Purging = regular bingeing or purging while maintaining BMI < 18.5. If a patient purges but has a BMI < 18.5, the diagnosis is ALWAYS Anorexia Nervosa (Binge/Purging type), NEVER Bulimia. Binge/Purging subtype carries severe risk of hypokalemia, cardiac arrhythmias, and metabolic alkalosis.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'The 3-Month Behavioral Evaluation', 'icon' => '🗓️', 'definition' => 'Subtype assignment reflects the presence or absence of bingeing/purging over the preceding 90 days.'],
            ['title' => 'BMI Overrides Bulimia', 'icon' => '⚖️', 'definition' => 'Any patient who binges and purges while at a significantly low weight (BMI < 18.5) is Anorexia Binge/Purge type.'],
            ['title' => 'Electrolyte & Purging Lethality', 'icon' => '⚡', 'definition' => 'Purging drives severe hypokalemia, hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis, and life-threatening ventricular arrhythmias.'],
            ['title' => 'Diagnostic Crossover Tendency', 'icon' => '🔄', 'definition' => 'Up to 50% of restricting anorexic patients cross over into the binge-eating/purging subtype over longitudinal follow-up.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Subjective Binge', 'definition' => 'An episode where an individual feels an intense loss of control while eating an amount of food that is objectively small or normal (e.g., eating an apple).'],
            ['term' => 'Objective Binge', 'definition' => 'Eating, in a discrete period of time, an amount of food that is definitely larger than what most people would eat under similar circumstances.'],
            ['term' => 'Metabolic Alkalosis', 'definition' => 'An arterial blood pH elevation resulting from gastric hydrochloric acid loss secondary to recurrent self-induced vomiting.'],
            ['term' => 'Hypokalemia', 'definition' => 'Abnormally low blood potassium concentration (< 3.5 mEq/L), the leading cause of lethal cardiac arrest in purging disorders.']
        ]
