    4 => [
        'title' => 'Bulimia Nervosa (Recurrent Bingeing & Compensatory Purging Cycles)',
        'slug' => 'bulimia-nervosa-criteria-compensatory-cycles',
        'description' => 'Objective binge criteria, lack of control, compensatory behaviors, weekly threshold (1x/week for 3 months), and normal/elevated BMI.',
        'overview' => "Bulimia Nervosa is an eating disorder characterized by recurrent episodes of binge eating, recurrent inappropriate compensatory behaviors to prevent weight gain, and self-evaluation that is unduly influenced by body shape and weight. Unlike patients with Anorexia Nervosa, individuals with Bulimia Nervosa maintain body weight at or above a minimally normal level (typically a BMI between 18.5 and 29.9 kg/m2, or in the overweight range).\n\nAn episode of binge eating is operationalized in DSM-5 by two mandatory features: (1) eating, in a discrete period of time (e.g., within any 2-hour period), an amount of food that is definitely larger than what most individuals would eat in a similar period under similar circumstances; and (2) a subjective sense of lack of control over eating during the episode (e.g., feeling that one cannot stop eating or control what or how much one is eating).\n\nIn DSM-5, the diagnostic frequency threshold was reduced from twice weekly in DSM-IV to at least once a week for at least three consecutive months. Bingeing is followed by intense shame, guilt, and panic regarding weight gain, driving compensatory behaviors such as self-induced vomiting, laxative abuse, fasting, or excessive exercise. Patients with Bulimia typically suffer in profound secrecy, maintaining outwardly normal lives while engaging in daily hidden purging cycles.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A: Recurrent episodes of binge eating. An episode of binge eating is characterized by both of the following:\n1. Eating, in a discrete period of time (e.g., within any 2-hour period), an amount of food that is definitely larger than what most individuals would eat in a similar period of time under similar circumstances.\n2. A sense of lack of control over eating during the episode (e.g., a feeling that one cannot stop eating or control what or how much one is eating).\n\nCriterion B: Recurrent inappropriate compensatory behaviors in order to prevent weight gain, such as self-induced vomiting; misuse of laxatives, diuretics, or other medications; fasting; or excessive exercise.\n\nCriterion C: The binge eating and inappropriate compensatory behaviors both occur, on average, at least once a week for 3 months.\n\nCriterion D: Self-evaluation is unduly influenced by body shape and weight.\n\nCriterion E: The disturbance does not occur exclusively during episodes of Anorexia Nervosa (i.e., body weight is at or above minimally normal level, BMI >= 18.5).\n\nDimensional Severity Tiers (Based on frequency of compensatory behaviors per week):\n• Mild: An average of 1–3 episodes of inappropriate compensatory behaviors per week.\n• Moderate: An average of 4–7 episodes per week.\n• Severe: An average of 8–13 episodes per week.\n• Extreme: An average of 14 or more episodes per week.",
        'specifiers' => "Clinicians assign current severity (Mild, Moderate, Severe, Extreme) based strictly on the weekly frequency of inappropriate compensatory behaviors. Remission specifiers include 'In partial remission' and 'In full remission'.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Bulimia Nervosa vs. Anorexia Nervosa, Binge-Eating/Purging Type: The definitive diagnostic test. If the patient is at a significantly low body weight (BMI < 18.5), the diagnosis is Anorexia Nervosa. Bulimia Nervosa requires a normal or elevated BMI (>= 18.5).\n\nBulimia Nervosa vs. Binge-Eating Disorder (BED): In BED, objective binges occur regularly, but compensatory purging behaviors (vomiting, laxatives, fasting) are ABSENT.\n\nBulimia vs. Kleine-Levin Syndrome: Kleine-Levin syndrome involves episodic hyperphagia paired with hypersomnia and hypersexuality, lacking body shape dissatisfaction.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 21-year-old college student of normal weight (height 5'6\", weight 135 lbs, BMI 21.8 kg/m2). For the past four months, the student has experienced episodes roughly four evenings per week where they consume large quantities of pizza, donuts, and cereal (3,500 calories in 90 minutes) while feeling completely unable to stop.\n\nImmediately following each binge, overcome by terror of weight gain and self-disgust, the student induces vomiting in the bathroom. The student's self-esteem is entirely dependent on keeping their stomach flat. Because bingeing and compensatory vomiting occur 4 times weekly for >3 months in a normal-weight individual, the diagnosis is confirmed as Bulimia Nervosa, Moderate Severity.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Bulimia Nervosa requires: (1) objective binges (large amount + loss of control), (2) compensatory behaviors (vomiting, laxatives, exercise), (3) frequency >= 1 time per week for 3 months, and (4) body weight at or above normal (BMI >= 18.5). DSM-5 lowered the frequency threshold from 2x/week to 1x/week. Severity is graded by weekly frequency of compensatory behaviors (Mild: 1-3, Moderate: 4-7, Severe: 8-13, Extreme: 14+). Fluoxetine (Prozac) at 60 mg is the only FDA-approved medication for Bulimia.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Normal Weight Habitus', 'icon' => '⚖️', 'definition' => 'Patients with Bulimia Nervosa maintain normal or elevated weight (BMI >= 18.5), distinguishing them from Anorexia.'],
            ['title' => 'The Once-Weekly Threshold', 'icon' => '⏱️', 'definition' => 'DSM-5 reduced the required frequency to at least 1 binge-purge cycle per week for at least 3 consecutive months.'],
            ['title' => 'Objective Binge Definition', 'icon' => '🍕', 'definition' => 'Requires both an objectively large quantity of food within a 2-hour window AND an undeniable sense of loss of control.'],
            ['title' => 'Compensatory Frequency Severity', 'icon' => '📊', 'definition' => 'Severity tiers (Mild to Extreme) are determined strictly by the number of compensatory purge episodes per week.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Inappropriate Compensatory Behavior', 'definition' => 'Maladaptive actions taken to prevent weight gain following eating, including self-induced vomiting, laxative abuse, and excessive exercise.'],
            ['term' => 'Loss of Control', 'definition' => 'A subjective psychological state during eating wherein an individual feels completely unable to stop eating or regulate food intake.'],
            ['term' => 'Fluoxetine 60 mg', 'definition' => 'The selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor (SSRI) with FDA approval for the treatment of Bulimia Nervosa, dosed higher than for depression.'],
            ['term' => 'Binge-Purge Cycle', 'definition' => 'A repetitive psychological loop wherein dietary restriction triggers physiological bingeing, which triggers panic and purging, restarting the cycle.']
        ]
