    5 => [
        'title' => 'Binge-Eating Disorder (BED: Objective Binges Without Compensation)',
        'slug' => 'binge-eating-disorder-criteria',
        'description' => 'Objective binges, 3 of 5 behavioral features, absence of compensatory behaviors, once a week for 3 months, and obesity associations.',
        'overview' => "Binge-Eating Disorder (BED) was officially established as a standalone diagnostic entity in DSM-5. In DSM-IV, BED was relegated to an appendix condition for further study under Eating Disorders Not Otherwise Specified (EDNOS). Extensive epidemiological and clinical neurobiological research demonstrated that BED is the most prevalent eating disorder in the general population, affecting approximately 2% to 3% of adults (with higher male representation than anorexia or bulimia) and carrying significant medical, metabolic, and psychiatric morbidity.\n\nThe core defining feature of Binge-Eating Disorder is recurrent episodes of binge eating characterized by consuming an objectively large amount of food within a discrete period of time accompanied by a subjective sense of loss of control, in the ABSOLUTE ABSENCE of recurrent inappropriate compensatory behaviors (such as self-induced vomiting, laxative misuse, or compensatory fasting).\n\nFurthermore, DSM-5 mandates that binge eating episodes must be associated with at least three of five specific behavioral markers reflecting uncontrolled consumption (such as eating much more rapidly than normal, eating until feeling uncomfortably full, or eating alone out of embarrassment). While BED is strongly associated with obesity and metabolic syndrome, BED is a distinct psychiatric disorder: the majority of obese individuals do not engage in recurrent binge eating.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A: Recurrent episodes of binge eating. An episode of binge eating is characterized by both of the following:\n1. Eating, in a discrete period of time (e.g., within any 2-hour period), an amount of food that is definitely larger than what most people would eat in a similar period under similar circumstances.\n2. A sense of lack of control over eating during the episode (e.g., a feeling that one cannot stop eating or control what or how much one is eating).\n\nCriterion B: The binge-eating episodes are associated with three (or more) of the following five features:\n1. Eating much more rapidly than normal.\n2. Eating until feeling uncomfortably full.\n3. Eating large amounts of food when not feeling physically hungry.\n4. Eating alone because of feeling embarrassed by how much one is eating.\n5. Feeling disgusted with oneself, depressed, or very guilty afterward.\n\nCriterion C: Marked distress regarding binge eating is present.\n\nCriterion D: The binge eating occurs, on average, at least once a week for 3 months.\n\nCriterion E: The binge eating is not associated with the recurrent use of inappropriate compensatory behavior as in bulimia nervosa and does not occur exclusively during the course of bulimia nervosa or anorexia nervosa.\n\nDimensional Severity Tiers (Based on frequency of binge episodes per week):\n• Mild: 1–3 binge eating episodes per week.\n• Moderate: 4–7 binge eating episodes per week.\n• Severe: 8–13 binge eating episodes per week.\n• Extreme: 14 or more binge eating episodes per week.",
        'specifiers' => "Severity is coded strictly by the weekly frequency of binge eating episodes (Mild: 1-3, Moderate: 4-7, Severe: 8-13, Extreme: 14+). Remission status: 'In partial remission' or 'In full remission'.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Binge-Eating Disorder vs. Bulimia Nervosa: The definitive board distinction. In Bulimia Nervosa, objective binges are followed by recurrent inappropriate compensatory behaviors (purging, fasting, laxatives); in BED, compensatory purging behaviors are ABSENT.\n\nBED vs. Obesity without Eating Disorder: Obese individuals without BED graze throughout the day or consume high-calorie meals without discrete episodes of objective bingeing accompanied by subjective loss of control and profound guilt.\n\nBED vs. Bipolar Depression / Atypical Depression: In atypical depression, hyperphagia represents passive overeating; in BED, overeating occurs in discrete, rapid paroxysms with loss of control.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 38-year-old male accountant (BMI 33.2 kg/m2) who reports distressing eating habits. Three nights per week, while his family is asleep, he sits in the kitchen and consumes two entire pizzas, a tub of ice cream, and a bag of chips in 45 minutes (eating rapidly, alone out of embarrassment, when not hungry).\n\nDuring these episodes, he feels completely helpless to stop eating. Afterward, he experiences overwhelming self-disgust, depression, and guilt. He has never induced vomiting, never used laxatives, and does not engage in compensatory fasting or exercise. Meeting criteria for objective binges with 4 associated features, occurring 3 times weekly for >3 months without compensation, confirms Binge-Eating Disorder, Mild Severity.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: BED requires objective binges (large amount + loss of control) occurring at least 1 time per week for 3 months with marked distress. Binges must feature at least 3 of 5 markers: (1) eating rapidly, (2) uncomfortably full, (3) eating when not hungry, (4) eating alone from embarrassment, (5) feeling disgusted/guilty. Crucially: Compensatory purging behaviors are ABSENT. BED was newly added to DSM-5. Lisdexamfetamine (Vyvanse) is the only FDA-approved medication for moderate-to-severe BED.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Standalone DSM-5 Classification', 'icon' => '🏛️', 'definition' => 'Elevated from DSM-IV appendix to full independent disorder; the most prevalent eating disorder in adults.'],
            ['title' => 'Absence of Compensatory Purging', 'icon' => '🚫', 'definition' => 'The definitive diagnostic test: objective binges occur WITHOUT vomiting, laxatives, or compensatory fasting.'],
            ['title' => 'The 3 of 5 Behavioral Cluster', 'icon' => '🔢', 'definition' => 'Requires eating rapidly, until uncomfortably full, when not hungry, alone from shame, and post-binge self-disgust.'],
            ['title' => 'Binge-Frequency Severity Grading', 'icon' => '📊', 'definition' => 'Severity tiers (Mild to Extreme) are determined by weekly binge episodes (unlike Bulimia which grades purges).']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Binge-Eating Disorder (BED)', 'definition' => 'An eating disorder characterized by recurrent episodes of eating large quantities of food with loss of control, without compensatory behaviors.'],
            ['term' => 'Lisdexamfetamine (Vyvanse)', 'definition' => 'A central nervous system stimulant and pro-drug of dextroamphetamine, the sole FDA-approved pharmacological treatment for moderate-to-severe BED.'],
            ['term' => 'Metabolic Syndrome', 'definition' => 'A cluster of biochemical and physiological abnormalities (hypertension, hyperglycemia, dyslipidemia) frequently comorbid with BED.'],
            ['term' => 'Grazing', 'definition' => 'The repetitive, continuous consumption of small amounts of food over extended periods without discrete episodic boundaries.']
        ]
    ],
    6 => [
        'title' => 'Medical Complications, Electrolyte Abnormalities, & Refeeding Syndrome',
        'slug' => 'medical-complications-electrolytes-refeeding-syndrome',
        'description' => 'Hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis, Russell\'s sign, parotid hypertrophy, and the lethal mechanism of refeeding syndrome (hypophosphatemia).',
        'overview' => "Eating disorders carry severe, multisystem medical complications that demand rigorous physiological monitoring and medical stabilization. The pathophysiology of eating disorder morbidity divides into two distinct mechanisms: the medical consequences of starvation and cachexia (seen in Anorexia Nervosa), and the medical consequences of repetitive purging (seen in Bulimia Nervosa and Anorexia Binge/Purge type).\n\nStarvation induces profound multisystem physiological shutdown: the body enters a hypometabolic state characterized by severe sinus bradycardia, hypotension, hypothermia, leukopenia, bone marrow gelatinous degeneration, osteopenia/osteoporosis, and down-regulation of the thyroid axis (Euthyroid Sick Syndrome with low free T3). In contrast, self-induced vomiting produces characteristic physical hallmarks: painless bilateral parotid salivary gland enlargement (sialadenosis), enamel erosion on the lingual surfaces of maxillary teeth (perimylolysis), and calluses or scars on the dorsal hand knuckles from contact with incisors (Russell's sign).\n\nAmong the most dangerous medical crises in eating disorder management is Refeeding Syndrome—a potentially fatal medical complication that occurs when calories (particularly carbohydrates) are reintroduced too rapidly to a severely malnourished individual. Refeeding triggers massive insulin surges that drive phosphorus, potassium, and magnesium into cells, causing precipitously lethal serum hypophosphatemia, cardiac arrhythmias, and heart failure.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Evaluating Medical Complications and Clinical Signs:\n\n1. Physical Signs of Purging:\n• Russell's Sign: Calluses, abrasions, or scars on the metacarpophalangeal joints and dorsal knuckles caused by friction against teeth during digital gag induction.\n• Dental Perimylolysis: Severe decalcification and lingual enamel erosion of teeth from repeated contact with acidic gastric secretions.\n• Parotid Sialadenosis: Bilateral, non-inflammatory hypertrophy of parotid salivary glands triggered by repeated vomiting-induced autonomic stimulation.\n\n2. Electrolyte Abnormalities from Self-Induced Vomiting:\n• Hypokalemia: Potassium < 3.5 mEq/L (due to gastric loss and renal aldosterone activation).\n• Hypochloremic Metabolic Alkalosis: Elevated serum bicarbonate and low chloride due to hydrogen and chloride loss in vomitus.\n• Laxative Abuse: Leads to non-anion gap metabolic ACIDOSIS and melanosis coli (dark brown pigmentation of the colon on colonoscopy).\n\n3. Refeeding Syndrome Criteria:\n• The hallmark biochemical marker is profound Hypophosphatemia (serum phosphate < 2.0 mg/dL).\n• Associated with hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, thiamine deficiency, peripheral edema, congestive heart failure, and seizures.",
        'specifiers' => "Documenting emergency hospitalization criteria: Heart rate < 40 bpm, blood pressure < 90/60 mmHg, QTc prolongation > 460 ms, potassium < 3.0 mEq/L, temperature < 35.5 C (96 F), or BMI < 15 kg/m2.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Vomiting vs. Laxative Abuse Electrolyte Signature: Self-induced vomiting produces metabolic ALKALOSIS (high bicarbonate, low chloride); laxative abuse produces metabolic ACIDOSIS (bicarbonate loss in diarrhea).\n\nRefeeding Hypophosphatemia vs. Renal Wasting: Refeeding hypophosphatemia occurs within 2 to 7 days of nutritional reintroduction due to intracellular shifting driven by insulin.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves an 18-year-old patient with severe anorexia (BMI 13.5 kg/m2) admitted to the hospital. The medical team rapidly starts high-calorie enteral feeds (2,500 kcal/day). On day 3 of refeeding, the patient develops severe dyspnea, peripheral edema, bilateral lung crackles, and confusion.\n\nStat laboratory testing reveals serum phosphorus of 1.1 mg/dL (profound hypophosphatemia) and potassium of 2.8 mEq/L. Electrocardiogram shows sinus tachycardia with frequent premature ventricular contractions. The patient is experiencing life-threatening Refeeding Syndrome, requiring immediate reduction of caloric intake, aggressive intravenous phosphate replacement, and cardiac telemetry monitoring.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Refeeding syndrome is driven by insulin surges driving phosphorus into cells, causing profound Hypophosphatemia, cardiac arrest, and delirium; prevent by 'starting low and going slow' and supplementing thiamine and phosphate. Vomiting produces Hypochloremic Metabolic Alkalosis; laxative abuse produces Metabolic Acidosis. Russell's sign = dorsal hand calluses from vomiting. Sialadenosis = swollen parotid glands. Bradycardia (<40 bpm) and BMI < 15 require immediate medical admission.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Refeeding Syndrome Mechanism', 'icon' => '🚨', 'definition' => 'Carbohydrate reintroduction triggers insulin surges driving phosphate, potassium, and magnesium intracellularly.'],
            ['title' => 'Hypophosphatemia as Hallmark', 'icon' => '🧪', 'definition' => 'Serum phosphate collapse (< 2.0 mg/dL) depletes ATP and 2,3-DPG, causing acute cardiac failure and diaphragmatic arrest.'],
            ['title' => 'Vomiting vs. Laxative Acid-Base Razor', 'icon' => '⚖️', 'definition' => 'Vomiting causes metabolic alkalosis (gastric acid loss); laxatives cause metabolic acidosis (fecal bicarbonate loss).'],
            ['title' => 'Classic Physical Stigmata', 'icon' => '🦷', 'definition' => 'Russell\'s sign (knuckle calluses), dental enamel perimylolysis, parotid sialadenosis, and lanugo body hair.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Refeeding Syndrome', 'definition' => 'A potentially fatal metabolic disturbance occurring upon nutritional reintroduction in starved patients, marked by profound hypophosphatemia and cardiac collapse.'],
            ['term' => 'Russell\'s Sign', 'definition' => 'Cutaneous abrasions, hyperkeratotic calluses, or scars on the knuckles and dorsum of the hand caused by manual gag reflex induction.'],
            ['term' => 'Perimylolysis', 'definition' => 'The irreversible erosion and chemical dissolution of dental enamel on the lingual surfaces of teeth caused by chronic gastric acid regurgitation.'],
            ['term' => 'Hypophosphatemia', 'definition' => 'Low blood phosphorus concentration (< 2.5 mg/dL), leading to ATP depletion, cardiac arrhythmias, muscle weakness, and hemolysis.']
        ]
    ],
    7 => [
        'title' => 'Enuresis: Diagnostic Criteria (Age 5+, 2x/Week for 3 Months)',
        'slug' => 'enuresis-diagnostic-criteria-subtypes',
        'description' => 'Involuntary voiding of urine, chronological age threshold (age 5+), frequency rules (2x/week for 3 months), and 3 clinical subtypes.',
        'overview' => "Enuresis is an elimination disorder characterized by the repeated voiding of urine into bed or clothes, whether involuntary or intentional. In DSM-5, elimination disorders are classified as a distinct diagnostic section closely linked to pediatric developmental milestones, neurobiological bladder innervation, and psychological stress.\n\nA fundamental diagnostic requirement is the developmental age milestone: the child must have reached a chronological age of at least 5 years (or an equivalent developmental level). Below age 5, nocturnal or diurnal urinary accidents are considered developmentally normal variations in bladder sphincter mastery and do not qualify for a psychiatric diagnosis.\n\nFurthermore, the voiding behavior must be clinically significant, operationalized as occurring with a minimum frequency of at least twice a week for at least three consecutive months (or causing clinically significant distress or functional impairment). Crucially, general medical etiologies—such as urinary tract infections, neurogenic bladder, diabetes mellitus (osmotic diuresis), or seizure disorders—must be ruled out through urinalysis before establishing a primary diagnosis of Enuresis.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A: Repeated voiding of urine into bed or clothes, whether involuntary or intentional.\n\nCriterion B: The behavior is clinically significant as manifested by either a frequency of at least twice a week for at least 3 consecutive months or the presence of clinically significant distress or impairment in social, academic (occupational), or other important areas of functioning.\n\nCriterion C: Chronological age is at least 5 years (or equivalent developmental level).\n\nCriterion D: The behavior is not attributable to the physiological effects of a substance (e.g., a diuretic, an antipsychotic medication) or another medical condition (e.g., diabetes, spina bifida, a seizure disorder).\n\nClinical Subtypes:\n1. 'Nocturnal only': Passage of urine only during nighttime sleep (the most common subtype, classically termed monosymptomatic enuresis or bedwetting; male predominance).\n2. 'Diurnal only': Passage of urine only during waking hours (often associated with urgency and deferral of micturition; female predominance).\n3. 'Nocturnal and diurnal': A combination of the two subtypes.",
        'specifiers' => "Clinicians specify subtype: 'Nocturnal only', 'Diurnal only', or 'Nocturnal and diurnal'.\n\nClinicians also record whether the condition is: 'Primary' (the child has never achieved urinary continence for at least 6 months) or 'Secondary' (the disorder emerges after an established period of at least 6 months of urinary continence, frequently precipitated by psychosocial stressors such as parental divorce or birth of a sibling).",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Enuresis vs. Urinary Tract Infection (UTI): A child with dysuria, urgency, and fever requires urinalysis and urine culture; UTI takes absolute medical precedence.\n\nEnuresis vs. Diabetes Mellitus Type 1: Polyuria and polydipsia presenting as new-onset secondary bedwetting in a school-aged child requires immediate blood glucose and urinalysis testing.\n\nEnuresis vs. Neurogenic Bladder: Spina bifida occulta, tethered spinal cord, or sacral agenesis produce neurogenic sphincter failure with abnormal lower extremity reflexes.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 6-year-old boy who wets his bed four nights per week. He has never had a dry period longer than two weeks in his life (Primary Enuresis). He never has daytime accidents and urinates normally at school.\n\nPhysical examination and urinalysis are completely normal, with no glucosuria, no proteinuria, and no bacterial infection. The child feels intense shame and refuses to attend sleepovers. Meeting criteria for voiding in bed >=2x/week for >3 months in a child over age 5, the diagnosis is confirmed as Enuresis, Nocturnal only (Primary). First-line behavioral treatment is the urine alarm (bell-and-pad method).",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Enuresis requires repeated voiding of urine with chronological age >= 5 years (not age 4). Frequency rule: at least twice a week for at least 3 consecutive months. Subtypes: Nocturnal only (most common; males), Diurnal only (females), and Nocturnal/Diurnal. Primary = never continent for 6 months; Secondary = continent for >= 6 months then relapsed (look for psychosocial stressor). First-line treatment: Urine alarm (bell-and-pad) conditioning; pharmacological backup: Desmopressin (DDAVP). Urinalysis is mandatory to rule out diabetes and UTI.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Age 5 Chronological Milestone', 'icon' => '👶', 'definition' => 'Cannot be diagnosed before chronological or developmental age 5 to exclude normal developmental delay in toilet training.'],
            ['title' => 'The 2x/Week for 3 Months Rule', 'icon' => '⏱️', 'definition' => 'Requires urinary voiding at least twice weekly for 3 consecutive months, or clinically significant impairment.'],
            ['title' => 'Three Phenomenological Subtypes', 'icon' => '🛏️', 'definition' => 'Nocturnal only (bedwetting), Diurnal only (daytime wetting), and Nocturnal and diurnal.'],
            ['title' => 'Urine Alarm Gold Standard', 'icon' => '⏰', 'definition' => 'The bell-and-pad moisture alarm represents the most effective, long-term conditioning intervention for nocturnal enuresis.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Nocturnal Enuresis', 'definition' => 'Involuntary voiding of urine occurring exclusively during nighttime sleep in a child aged 5 years or older.'],
            ['term' => 'Bell-and-Pad Method (Urine Alarm)', 'definition' => 'A behavioral conditioning device that sounds an auditory alarm upon detecting initial drops of urine, conditioning nocturnal sphincter contraction.'],
            ['term' => 'Desmopressin (DDAVP)', 'definition' => 'A synthetic analogue of vasopressin (antidiuretic hormone) that reduces nocturnal urine volume, used for temporary management of enuresis.'],
            ['term' => 'Primary vs. Secondary Enuresis', 'definition' => 'Primary indicates urinary continence was never achieved for 6 months; secondary indicates enuresis emerged after at least 6 months of continence.']
        ]
    ],
    8 => [
        'title' => 'Encopresis: Diagnostic Criteria (Age 4+, 1x/Month for 3 Months)',
        'slug' => 'encopresis-diagnostic-criteria-subtypes',
        'description' => 'Inappropriate passage of feces, chronological age threshold (age 4+), frequency rule (1x/month for 3 months), and constipation/overflow incontinence.',
        'overview' => "Encopresis is an elimination disorder characterized by the repeated passage of feces into inappropriate places (e.g., clothing, floor), whether involuntary or intentional. Like enuresis, encopresis is fundamentally tied to neurodevelopmental milestones and bowel sphincter mastery.\n\nA central diagnostic requirement is the chronological age threshold: the child must have reached a chronological age of at least 4 years (or an equivalent developmental level). Below age 4, fecal accidents are considered developmentally normative aspects of toddler bowel training. The diagnostic frequency threshold requires at least one encopresis event per month for at least three consecutive months.\n\nA crucial pathophysiological distinction in DSM-5 divides Encopresis into two clinical subtypes: 'With constipation and overflow incontinence' and 'Without constipation and overflow incontinence'. The overwhelming majority of encopresis cases (over 80% to 90%) involve constipation and overflow incontinence. In this physiological subtype, chronic stool withholding causes massive fecal impaction in the rectum; over time, the rectal vault stretches, losing sensory elasticity, and semi-liquid fecal matter leaks around the hard impaction involuntarily onto clothing.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Criterion A: Repeated passage of feces into inappropriate places (e.g., underwear, floor), whether involuntary or intentional.\n\nCriterion B: At least one such event occurs each month for at least 3 months.\n\nCriterion C: Chronological age is at least 4 years (or equivalent developmental level).\n\nCriterion D: The behavior is not attributable to the physiological effects of a substance (e.g., laxatives) or another medical condition except through a mechanism involving constipation.\n\nClinical Subtypes:\n1. 'With constipation and overflow incontinence': There is clinical history or physical examination evidence of constipation (e.g., infrequent bowel movements, firm stool, abdominal distension, rectal impaction). Feces are typically poorly formed, and leakage is continuous, occurring mostly during daytime hours.\n2. 'Without constipation and overflow incontinence': There is NO clinical or physical evidence of constipation. Feces are usually of normal form and consistency, and passage is intermittent, frequently associated with Oppositional Defiant Disorder or Conduct Disorder.",
        'specifiers' => "Clinicians must specify subtype: 'With constipation and overflow incontinence' or 'Without constipation and overflow incontinence'.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Encopresis With Overflow Incontinence vs. Diarrhea: Parents frequently misinterpret overflow leakage as acute diarrhea and administer anti-diarrheal medications, which catastrophically worsens the underlying fecal impaction; abdominal examination or KUB x-ray reveals severe impaction.\n\nEncopresis vs. Hirschsprung Disease: Hirschsprung disease involves congenital aganglionosis of the distal colon presenting in the neonatal period with failure to pass meconium; encopresis is functional and developmental.\n\nEncopresis Without Constipation vs. Conduct Disorder: Deliberate fecal soiling in closets or beds without constipation is frequently a manifestation of severe oppositional defiance or conduct pathology.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 5-year-old boy whose parents report daily liquid fecal soiling in his underwear for four months. The parents complain the child is 'too lazy to go to the toilet' and note he has firm bowel movements only once a week.\n\nAbdominal palpation reveals a palpable, hard fecal mass in the left lower quadrant, and digital rectal exam reveals hard impaction with liquid stool leakage. The child reports he cannot feel when the soiling happens due to rectal desensitization. Meeting criteria for fecal passage into clothing >1x/month for >3 months at age 5 with constipation, the diagnosis is Encopresis, With constipation and overflow incontinence. Treatment requires medical bowel disimpaction followed by stool softeners and behavioral toilet scheduling.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: Encopresis requires chronological age >= 4 years (compare to Enuresis which requires age >= 5). Frequency rule: at least 1 event per month for at least 3 consecutive months. Over 85% of cases are 'With constipation and overflow incontinence'. Liquid soiling around a hard fecal mass is NOT diarrhea—never give anti-diarrheals. Encopresis 'Without constipation' involves normal stool consistency and is linked to oppositional or conduct pathology. Medical disimpaction is the first step.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Age 4 Chronological Milestone', 'icon' => '👶', 'definition' => 'Cannot be diagnosed before age 4 (remember: Encopresis = Age 4; Enuresis = Age 5).'],
            ['title' => 'The 1x/Month for 3 Months Rule', 'icon' => '⏱️', 'definition' => 'Requires at least one inappropriate fecal passage event per month for 3 consecutive months.'],
            ['title' => 'Overflow Incontinence Predominance', 'icon' => '💩', 'definition' => 'Over 85% of cases result from chronic constipation and fecal impaction with involuntary liquid bypass.'],
            ['title' => 'Medical Disimpaction Priority', 'icon' => '💊', 'definition' => 'Behavioral modification will fail without initial complete medical evacuation of the impacted rectal vault.']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Fecal Impaction', 'definition' => 'A large, hard mass of dried stool stuck in the rectum that cannot be evacuated naturally, driving overflow incontinence.'],
            ['term' => 'Stool Withholding', 'definition' => 'A voluntary behavioral resistance to defecating, often triggered by past painful hard bowel movements, initiating chronic constipation loops.'],
            ['term' => 'Megarectum', 'definition' => 'Chronic dilation and loss of elasticity of the rectal vault resulting from prolonged fecal retention, dampening normal defecatory urges.'],
            ['term' => 'Encopresis', 'definition' => 'The repeated passage of feces into inappropriate places in an individual aged 4 years or older, occurring at least once monthly for 3 months.']
        ]
    ],
    9 => [
        'title' => 'Differential Diagnosis: Anorexia vs. Bulimia vs. BED vs. OCD vs. Medical Causes',
        'slug' => 'differential-diagnosis-eating-elimination-disorders',
        'description' => 'Systematic differential algorithm: BMI gateway, compensatory purge presence, objective binge verification, and OCD boundary.',
        'overview' => "The differential diagnosis of Feeding, Eating, and Elimination Disorders requires commanding a precise, sequential clinical decision tree. Because patients across the eating disorder spectrum share overlapping preoccupations with food, weight, shape, and ritualistic behaviors, clinical misdiagnosis is frequent unless objective diagnostic gateways are strictly applied.\n\nThe initial, non-negotiable gateway in evaluating any eating presentation is Body Mass Index (BMI). If an individual is at a significantly low body weight (BMI < 18.5 in adults, or < 5th percentile in children), the diagnosis MUST be Anorexia Nervosa (Restricting or Binge-Eating/Purging type). Under DSM-5 rules, an individual with a BMI of 16.0 who binges and purges daily can NEVER be diagnosed with Bulimia Nervosa—they are diagnosed with Anorexia Nervosa, Binge-Eating/Purging Type.\n\nIf the individual maintains a normal or elevated body weight (BMI >= 18.5), the clinician assesses for compensatory behaviors: if objective binges are followed by compensatory purging -> Bulimia Nervosa; if objective binges occur WITHOUT compensatory purging -> Binge-Eating Disorder. If food restriction occurs WITHOUT body image distortion or fear of fatness -> ARFID.\n\nSimilarly, elimination disorders require strict adherence to chronological age gates: Encopresis requires age >= 4; Enuresis requires age >= 5.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "The Sequential Diagnostic Algorithm:\n\n1. Step 1: Body Mass Index (BMI) Gateway:\n• BMI < 18.5 kg/m2 + Fear of fatness + Body distortion -> ANOREXIA NERVOSA.\n  - No binge/purge in last 3 months -> Restricting Type.\n  - Bingeing or purging in last 3 months -> Binge-Eating/Purging Type.\n\n2. Step 2: Normal/Elevated Weight Habitus (BMI >= 18.5):\n• Objective Binges + Inappropriate Compensatory Purges (>= 1x/wk for 3 mos) -> BULIMIA NERVOSA.\n• Objective Binges + NO Compensatory Purges (>= 1x/wk for 3 mos) -> BINGE-EATING DISORDER.\n\n3. Step 3: Food Restriction WITHOUT Fear of Fatness/Body Distortion:\n• Nutritional failure/weight loss driven by sensory/choking fears -> ARFID.\n\n4. Step 4: Elimination Age Milestones:\n• Inappropriate feces (>= 1x/mo for 3 mos) + Age >= 4 -> ENCOPRESIS.\n• Inappropriate urine (>= 2x/wk for 3 mos) + Age >= 5 -> ENURESIS.",
        'specifiers' => "Diagnostic formulations specify primary eating/elimination diagnoses, severity tiers (BMI for Anorexia; Purge frequency for Bulimia; Binge frequency for BED), and subtype classifications.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Eating Disorders vs. Gastrointestinal Disease (Crohn's, Celiac, Achalasia): Patients with organic GI disease lose weight but desire to gain weight and do not fear fatness.\n\nEating Rituals in OCD vs. Anorexia: In OCD, food rituals (symmetry, checking expiration dates) are driven by contamination or magical thinking; in Anorexia, rituals are driven by caloric restriction and fear of fatness.",
        'clinical_example' => "A hypothetical clinical vignette involves a 20-year-old student (height 5'7\", weight 98 lbs, BMI 15.3 kg/m2, Severe Anorexia range) who binges on large quantities of food three times weekly, followed by self-induced vomiting.\n\nApplying the algorithm: Step 1 evaluates BMI. Because BMI is 15.3 (significantly low weight), the diagnosis is immediately captured by Anorexia Nervosa, Binge-Eating/Purging Type, Severe Severity. The candidate rejects Bulimia Nervosa, recognizing that low BMI overrides Bulimia.",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield licensure board points: The BMI gateway: BMI < 18.5 + body distortion = Anorexia Nervosa (Binge/Purge type if purging). Normal weight (BMI >= 18.5) + bingeing + purging = Bulimia Nervosa. Normal/overweight + bingeing + NO purging = Binge-Eating Disorder. Food restriction + NO body distortion = ARFID. Encopresis = Age >= 4; Enuresis = Age >= 5. Remember: Anorexia always trumps Bulimia when body weight is significantly low.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'The BMI Absolute Gateway', 'icon' => '⚖️', 'definition' => 'BMI < 18.5 defines Anorexia Nervosa, immediately excluding Bulimia Nervosa regardless of purge frequency.'],
            ['title' => 'Compensatory Purge Differentiation', 'icon' => '🔄', 'definition' => 'Presence of regular purges separates Bulimia from Binge-Eating Disorder in normal/overweight patients.'],
            ['title' => 'The ARFID Non-Cosmetic Boundary', 'icon' => '🚫', 'definition' => 'Absence of drive for thinness and body image distortion separates ARFID from Anorexia Nervosa.'],
            ['title' => 'Elimination Age Milestone Rules', 'icon' => '👶', 'definition' => 'Exact command over developmental age thresholds: Encopresis (Age 4) vs. Enuresis (Age 5).']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Diagnostic Gateway', 'definition' => 'A primary clinical criterion (such as BMI < 18.5) that must be evaluated first to determine the categorical branch of diagnosis.'],
            ['term' => 'Cachexia', 'definition' => 'Severe physical wasting, loss of weight, and muscle atrophy resulting from chronic starvation or advanced medical illness.'],
            ['term' => 'Diagnostic Primacy', 'definition' => 'The nosological rule establishing that one disorder takes absolute precedence over another when overlapping symptoms occur (e.g., Anorexia over Bulimia).'],
            ['term' => 'Compensatory Purging', 'definition' => 'Behaviors executed specifically to counteract the caloric impact of food ingestion, absent in Binge-Eating Disorder.']
        ]
    ],
    10 => [
        'title' => 'Chapter Mastery & Comprehensive Board Review',
        'slug' => 'chapter-mastery-board-review-eating-elimination',
        'description' => 'Comprehensive synthesis of Feeding, Eating, and Elimination Disorders, diagnostic criteria, and high-yield board pearls.',
        'overview' => "This capstone unit delivers an advanced synthesis of Feeding, Eating, and Elimination Disorders under DSM-5 criteria, consolidating core diagnostic formulations, duration thresholds, and high-yield board pearls for professional licensure examinations. Eating and elimination pathology represents heavily tested content across clinical examinations, demanding exact command over diagnostic criteria, specifiers, and differential boundaries.\n\nMastery requires commanding the clinical distinctions separating Anorexia Nervosa, Bulimia Nervosa, Binge-Eating Disorder, ARFID, Pica, Rumination Disorder, Enuresis, and Encopresis. Furthermore, candidates must navigate DSM-5 structural refinements, including the elimination of the amenorrhea requirement in Anorexia, the reduction of binge-purge frequency to once weekly, the establishment of BED and ARFID as independent disorders, and the age milestones of elimination conditions.\n\nBy synthesizing clinical vignettes, diagnostic decision matrices, and medical complication emergencies, this unit prepares students and clinicians for board examination excellence and compassionate clinical practice.",
        'diagnostic_criteria' => "Mastery Summary of Core Eating and Elimination Rules:\n\n1. Anorexia Nervosa: Restriction leading to significantly low weight (BMI < 18.5); intense fear of gaining weight; body image distortion. Subtypes: Restricting vs. Binge-Eating/Purging (last 3 mos). BMI Severity: Mild (>=17), Moderate (16-16.99), Severe (15-15.99), Extreme (<15). Amenorrhea NOT required.\n\n2. Bulimia Nervosa: Objective binges + inappropriate compensatory behaviors >= 1 time/week for 3 months; normal/elevated weight (BMI >= 18.5). Severity based on purge frequency (Mild: 1-3, Moderate: 4-7, Severe: 8-13, Extreme: 14+ purges/wk).\n\n3. Binge-Eating Disorder: Objective binges >= 1 time/week for 3 months with 3 of 5 behavioral features; NO compensatory behaviors. Severity based on binge frequency.\n\n4. ARFID: Food restriction/avoidance leading to weight loss, nutritional deficiency, supplement dependence WITHOUT fear of weight gain or body image distortion.\n\n5. Enuresis: Inappropriate urine voiding >= 2x/week for 3 months; chronological age >= 5 years. Subtypes: Nocturnal, Diurnal, Nocturnal/Diurnal.\n\n6. Encopresis: Inappropriate fecal passage >= 1x/month for 3 months; chronological age >= 4 years. Subtypes: With vs. Without constipation/overflow incontinence.",
        'specifiers' => "Review of essential specifiers: Subtypes in Anorexia (Restricting vs. Binge/Purge); Severity tiers (BMI in Anorexia; Purge count in Bulimia; Binge count in BED); Subtypes in Enuresis and Encopresis.",
        'differential_diagnosis' => "Key Board Differential Pearls:\n• Patient purges daily but has BMI 16.0 -> Anorexia Nervosa, Binge-Eating/Purging Type (BMI < 18.5 overrides Bulimia).\n• Patient purges daily with BMI 22.0 -> Bulimia Nervosa.\n• Patient binges 3x weekly with BMI 32.0 and NO purging -> Binge-Eating Disorder.\n• Child refuses solid food after choking, loses weight, no body distortion -> ARFID.\n• Bedwetting in a 6-year-old 3x weekly -> Enuresis (Age >= 5, 2x/wk for 3 mos).\n• Fecal soiling in a 5-year-old with hard impaction -> Encopresis, With constipation and overflow incontinence (Age >= 4).\n• Refeeding syndrome = Hypophosphatemia triggered by rapid refeeding.\n• Vomiting = Metabolic Alkalosis; Laxatives = Metabolic Acidosis.",
        'clinical_example' => "A comprehensive clinical review scenario: A board examination vignette describes a 16-year-old high school gymnast who weighs 88 pounds at 5'4\" (BMI 15.1 kg/m2). The gymnast restricts food to 300 calories during the day, but three times a week consumes large amounts of cereal and induces vomiting. The gymnast has not had a menstrual period in four months.\n\nBoard Exam Question Formulation: What is the diagnosis and severity? The candidate identifies that significantly low body weight (BMI 15.1) immediately establishes Anorexia Nervosa, the purging over the past three months establishes Binge-Eating/Purging Type, and BMI between 15.0 and 15.99 confirms Severe Severity: Anorexia Nervosa, Binge-Eating/Purging Type, Severe (noting amenorrhea is no longer a diagnostic criterion).",
        'summary_notes' => "High-yield board pearls summary: Anorexia = BMI < 18.5 (low weight trumps Bulimia). Bulimia = normal weight + purge >= 1x/wk for 3 mos. BED = binges + NO purges. ARFID = restriction WITHOUT body image distortion. Enuresis = Age >= 5 (2x/wk for 3 mos). Encopresis = Age >= 4 (1x/mo for 3 mos). Refeeding syndrome = lethal Hypophosphatemia. Vomiting = hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis. Laxatives = metabolic acidosis. Fluoxetine = Bulimia; Lisdexamfetamine = BED.",
        'key_concepts' => [
            ['title' => 'Eating & Elimination Master Matrix', 'icon' => '🏛️', 'definition' => 'Comprehensive command of BMI thresholds, purge frequencies, and age milestones across all eating/elimination disorders.'],
            ['title' => 'The Anorexia Primacy Rule', 'icon' => '⚖️', 'definition' => 'BMI < 18.5 automatically establishes Anorexia Nervosa, excluding Bulimia regardless of binge/purge severity.'],
            ['title' => 'Refeeding Hypophosphatemia Emergency', 'icon' => '🧪', 'definition' => 'Understanding insulin-driven intracellular phosphate shifting and implementing gradual caloric reintroduction protocols.'],
            ['title' => 'Elimination Age Demarcation', 'icon' => '👶', 'definition' => 'Commanding the developmental age boundaries: Encopresis (Age 4) vs. Enuresis (Age 5).']
        ],
        'important_terms' => [
            ['term' => 'Eating Disorder Spectrum', 'definition' => 'The continuum of psychiatric conditions defined by disturbances in eating behaviors, nutritional intake, and body image evaluation.'],
            ['term' => 'Refeeding Syndrome', 'definition' => 'A potentially fatal shift in fluid and electrolytes that occurs in malnourished patients receiving artificial refeeding, characterized by hypophosphatemia.'],
            ['term' => 'Sialadenosis', 'definition' => 'Bilateral, painless enlargement of the parotid salivary glands classically observed in purging eating disorders.'],
